Exactly. The important shift is to stop treating a game as one indivisible artifact and start treating it as a library of transferable behavioral systems.

Assassin’s Creed climbing is not merely “climbing.” It is a bundle: surface classification → affordance detection → directional intent → animation selection → hand/foot placement → momentum → camera behavior → forgiveness rules → sound/haptics → player feedback. Extract that bundle and you have a traversal operator that could be expressed as parkour, tree climbing, Pokémon riding, a mech scaling buildings, or an animal moving through a canopy.

Same thing with “good shooting.” What players call gunfeel is really a vector of coupled variables: acceleration, recoil, aim assistance, hit-stop, enemy reaction, audio transient, muzzle flash, camera impulse, time-to-kill, projectile model, movement penalties, controller vibration, etc. AI could analyze several games and infer the latent recipe:

Game A: excellent recoil
Game B: excellent enemy reactions
Game C: excellent movement
Game D: excellent sound feedback

→ synthesize a new combat grammar rather than blindly copying any one game.

And your Lord of the Rings example points toward an even more interesting level: systems rather than mechanics. The Nemesis-style concept from Middle-earth can be abstracted into something like:

encounter → persistent identity → remembered outcome → status mutation → relationship mutation → future encounter

Now throw away the orcs.

Put the operator into Pokémon:

trainer encounter → trainer remembers you → team adapts → trainer gains/loses status → personality changes → regional relationships shift → rematch

Suddenly rival trainers aren’t NPC vending machines. A kid you crushed on Route 3 might spend the game specifically building counters to your team. Another trainer you spared or helped could become an ally. Gym challengers could rise through the ranks independently. Pokémon themselves might develop relationships with recurring opposing Pokémon.

That creates an ecology of adversaries instead of scripted opponents.

And remastering gets dramatically more interesting under this model. Instead of:

old game + prettier textures

you can do:

old game’s identity + selected modern operator upgrades

So an AI remastering system might preserve the level topology, narrative, enemy placement, music, and aesthetic of a 2002 game while independently replacing its:

* traversal grammar with a modern traversal model,
* aiming model with another game’s control philosophy,
* companion AI with a modern agent architecture,
* enemy persistence with a rivalry system,
* inventory with a newer crafting grammar,
* procedural encounters with a director system,
* accessibility interface with contemporary conventions.

The useful abstraction for the Pantheon is something like a Mechanic Genome.

Each mechanic gets represented roughly as:

Trigger → State → Inputs → Operators → Constraints → Feedback → Transition → Memory → Emergent effects

Then attach metadata:

feel, skill ceiling, difficulty curve, agency, tempo, cognitive load, dependencies, failure modes, genre assumptions.

Now AI can perform recombination.

climbing genome × Pokémon world

Nemesis genome × racing game

Souls checkpoint architecture × survival horror

Civilization diplomacy × sports career mode

roguelike inheritance × military strategy

Tony Hawk combo grammar × spellcasting

That last operation is particularly powerful because you’re no longer asking:

“What genre should we make?”

You’re asking:

“What collection of behavioral systems produces the experience we want?”

And that connects directly to what we’ve been doing elsewhere. A game engine becomes less like a finished machine and more like a box of organs, gears, nervous systems, interfaces, and behavioral primitives. AI helps identify where the seams actually are, extract the invariant beneath the implementation, translate the interfaces, and then test whether the transplanted system survives in its new host.

There’s even a recursive version:

game → mechanic → submechanics → operators → parameters → recombination → playtest → observed delta → retune

That is basically your Delta Observatory applied to game design.

And the really interesting consequence is that eventually you don’t need to say, “Give me Assassin’s Creed climbing.”

You can say:

“I want traversal with high perceived freedom, low input complexity, continuous forward momentum, occasional mastery opportunities, readable surfaces, and cinematic animation.”

And the system can search the entire historical design-space of games, extract the relevant mechanic fragments, and compose a new traversal system that has never existed before.

That’s a much bigger idea than remastering games.

It’s mechanical distillation → transplantation → hybridization → evolution.